+\begin{proof}[Proof of the horizontal monomorphisms]
+It suffices to prove that an $\mathbb S$-definable bijection
+$F:I^\sharp\fun J^\sharp$ in $\RV[n]$ implies that $I$ and $J$ are
+isomorphic in $\Gamma[n]$, retaining all finite disjoint-union tags.
+The case $n=0$ is immediate, so suppose $n>0$.
+
+For each $\alpha\in I$, the full fibre $\alpha^\sharp$ is a torsor
+for $(\K^\times)^n$ and has a unique residue-generic type. By
+residue-field/value-group orthogonality, $\vrv\circ F$ has finite
+image on that fibre, with its target tag included. Exactly one target
+fibre $\beta^\sharp$ receives its generic part. Its image has residue
+dimension $n$, since $F$ is injective, and is therefore generic in
+$\beta^\sharp$. The same argument for $F^{-1}$ shows that this
+assignment defines a bijection $h:I\fun J$. Definability of residue
+dimension in families makes $h$ definable over $\mathbb S$.
+After trivializing the two fibres solely for the proof, the generic
+restriction of $F$ is a birational map of residue tori: in
+characteristic zero, the constructible maps supplied by $F$ and
+$F^{-1}$ are rational inverses on dense open subsets.
+
+Fix $\alpha\in I$, put $\beta=h(\alpha)$, and set
+\[
+ \Delta=\vrv(\RV\cap\dcl(\mathbb S)),\qquad
+ L=(\Delta+\textstyle\sum_i\Z\alpha_i+\sum_i\Z\beta_i)/\Delta.
+\]
+The group $L$ is finitely generated. Let $D$ be the diagonalizable
+group over $\K$ with character group $L$. Its two diagonal
+representations have weights $\bar\alpha_i$ and $\bar\beta_i$.
+Every character $\chi:L\fun\K^\times$ extends to $\Gamma/\Delta$,
+since $\K^\times$ is divisible. The resulting RV twist
+$u\efun\chi(\vrv(u))u$ fixes the residue field, every Gamma value,
+and every old-ground RV point. It preserves the RV group operations
+and residue addition, so relative RV quantifier elimination makes
+it preserve every $\mathbb S$-definable RV relation, including the
+graph of $F$. The birational map of the two residue tori is thus
+$D$-equivariant. The two actions have the same kernel, by that map
+and its inverse, and their joint kernel is trivial because all their
+weights generate $L$. Both representations are faithful.
+
+By \cite[Theorem~7.1 and Corollary~2.5]{reichstein:youssin:birational},
+there is a matrix $M\in\operatorname{GL}_n(\Z)$ with
+$\beta-M\alpha\in\Delta^n$. Choose
+$c_i\in\RV\cap\dcl(\mathbb S)$ with these values. Then
+\[
+ H(u)_i=c_i\prod_j u_j^{M_{ij}}
+\]
+is an $\mathbb S$-definable bijection of the full fibres at $\alpha$
+and $\beta$. This replaces the generic birational map; it need not
+extend the given map on its dense open domain. Compactness supplies
+finitely many such formulas on a definable partition of $I$.
+Their full-preimage maps glue to a bijection contracting to $h$,
+so $h$ is a $\Gamma[n]$-morphism. This proves the lower horizontal
+positive injection. The upper injection follows from the commuting
+square and the two vertical positive injections.
+\end{proof}
